---
/*
  The only chrome on the site.

  A MENU label sits in the flow at the top of the page and scrolls away with it:
  no persistent bar competing with the imagery (DESIGN-PLAN section 1). Opening
  it covers the screen with a flat panel of labels. The language switch lives
  here, so it is always one tap away without occupying a corner of every screen.

  Built on <details>, so it opens, closes and takes keyboard focus with no
  JavaScript at all. When open, the summary becomes the fixed CLOSE control
  above the panel; that is a CSS state change, not motion.

  A DESIGNED MOMENT, not a list of links (2026-08-03). Three things make it one,
  and each is something the site already owns rather than something new:

  1. IT INVERTS. The panel is the opposite polarity to the page under it, so
     opening the menu performs the site's one structural idea in miniature: the
     document has two states. On a paper page the menu is ink, and the reverse.
     No fade, no slide: the screen simply becomes its opposite.

     SINCE SECTION 82 THIS IS THE ONLY INVERSION LEFT ON THE SITE. Every page is
     paper, so the panel is always ink — and that makes this the one place a
     reader still meets the other polarity. It is not a leftover; it is what a
     menu opening over a page should do, and it is now the whole of the site's
     two-state idea rather than a miniature of it.
  2. THE SPINE RUNS THROUGH IT, because it is a text surface and that is where
     the spine belongs (section 2). It is the only vertical line on the panel.
  3. IT IS DESTINATIONS AND NOTHING ELSE. It briefly carried the address and the
     Instagram handle as mono facts, on the argument that a reader opening a menu
     is often looking for a person; the flat menu (s67) took them out, CONTACT
     being one tap away, and this comment went on describing them until section
     84. The Instagram destination is the only settings value the panel reads.

  ONE SIZE, NO RULES (2026-08-04). The destinations were at display size with a
  hairline under each; the reference sets its whole menu at 11px with nothing
  between the items. Underline on hover is the only state, because a rule per
  row is six rules a reader has to look past.
*/
import Signature from "./Signature.astro";
import LanguageSwitch from "./LanguageSwitch.astro";
import Spine from "./Spine.astro";
import {getSiteSettings} from "../lib/content";
import {localePath, type Locale} from "../lib/locales";
import {t, MENU_DESTINATIONS} from "../i18n/ui";

interface Props {
  locale: Locale;
  /** The polarity of the page this menu sits on; the panel takes the other. */
  theme?: "light" | "dark";
}

const {locale, theme = "light"} = Astro.props;
const panelTheme = theme === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";

const settings = await getSiteSettings();
/*
  The panel carries INSTAGRAM as a destination and nothing else from settings.
  The address and the handle were read here when the menu still printed contact
  details; the flat menu (s67) removed them and the reads stayed until 2026-08-11.
*/
const instagram = settings.instagramUrl?.trim() || null;
---

<details class="menu">
  <summary class="type-label text-label menu-toggle" aria-label={t(locale, "menu")}>
    <span class="menu-when-closed">{t(locale, "menu")}</span>
    {/*
      The close control sits OVER the inverted panel, so it takes the panel's
      polarity rather than the page's. Without this it is ink on ink: the label
      was invisible the first time this was rendered and looked at.
    */}
    <span class="menu-when-open text-fg" data-theme={panelTheme}>{t(locale, "close")}</span>
  </summary>

  <div class="menu-panel bg-bg text-fg frame" data-theme={panelTheme}>
    <Spine />

    {/*
      GROUP ONE: where a reader can go. Tight leading, so six entries read as
      one block rather than six decisions.
    */}
    <nav class="flex flex-col gap-u2" aria-label={t(locale, "menu")}>
      {
        MENU_DESTINATIONS.map(({key, path}) => (
          <a href={localePath(locale, path)} class="type-label text-label hover:underline underline-offset-8">
            {t(locale, key)}
          </a>
        ))
      }
      {/*
        THE CART IS NOT HERE (2026-08-16, section 129), and it was, for a day.
        Section 126 added a seventh entry with the count beside it, because the
        order page had nowhere else to live. It has one now: the chrome carries
        the cart on every page, always visible, which is what the OWNER asked
        for and what puts a count where a reader can see it without opening
        anything. A menu entry as well would be the same destination twice, and
        the flat menu of six is a settled decision (s67).
      */}
    </nav>

    {/*
      GROUP TWO: where a reader can go that is not this site, and the language.
      Separated from group one by whitespace and nothing else. The email left:
      it is an address, not a destination, and it lives on Contact.
    */}
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-u8">
      <nav class="flex flex-col gap-u2" aria-label={t(locale, "instagram")}>
        {instagram && (
          <a
            href={instagram}
            rel="me noopener"
            target="_blank"
            class="type-label text-label hover:underline underline-offset-8"
          >
            {t(locale, "instagram")}
          </a>
        )}
        <LanguageSwitch locale={locale} />
      </nav>

      <div class="flex items-end justify-between gap-u3">
        <a href={localePath(locale)} aria-label={t(locale, "home")} class="block">
          <Signature class="h-sig-corner" decorative />
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</details>
